<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <RootNamespace>Domain</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <!--
    THE FIRST InternalsVisibleTo IN THIS SOLUTION'S HISTORY, and it is granted to exactly one assembly
    for exactly one member.

    NarrativeField.FromStore rebuilds a sealed value from bytes a column already holds, without judging
    them. Materialising a narrative column needs it and nothing else does. It is internal because a
    public door that skips the rule IS the hole the type was built to close: a way to put unjudged bytes
    into a narrative column, on a call site that reads like bookkeeping. The alternative to this grant is
    making that member public — a member every ring could reach so that one of them could — and the
    mistake it would let through is the invisible kind, since a row holding plaintext is a well-formed
    row that reads back, violates nothing and simply hands the operator the ledger.

    The grant is narrower than the outward edge a reader will fear: it names Infrastructure alone, so
    Application, Api and both test projects still cannot call FromStore. Nothing about it inverts the
    Dependency Rule — Domain still references nothing, and Infrastructure already references Domain
    transitively. What travels is visibility, not a dependency.

    A GUARD SEES IT NOW, AND IT IS STILL WORTH REVIEWING. ProjectReferenceGraphTests was taught this
    item type: the renderer emits one row per grant — "Domain: internals Infrastructure" — and that row
    is in the pinned edge set, so a second InternalsVisibleTo beside this one reddens the pin by name
    rather than arriving with nothing red. One row per grant and never a count or a flag, because
    "Domain grants its internals to somebody" would stay true while the somebody changed.

    What the guard holds is the SET of grants, not the wisdom of any of them. It cannot tell that
    Infrastructure is the right assembly, that FromStore is the right member, or that a future grant is
    a mistake — a reviewer widening the pinned set in the same commit passes it. So the argument above
    is what decides whether a grant belongs here, and this file is still where that argument is read.
    The guard's job is only to make sure nobody has to notice the line to be told it moved.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Infrastructure" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
